 said no to Theresa May A's  backstop option are you at the point  where the dup regrets saying yes to  brexit in the first place well we say  yes to power sharing but it has to be on  the basis of mutual respect it has to be  on the basis of adhering to the  principles of the Belfast or Good Friday  agreement and at the heart of that  agreement is that Northern Ireland is  part of the United Kingdom that was even  accepted by the Irish government who  changed their constitution to recognize  Northern Ireland as part of the UK the  protocol has cut across that we never  supported that the protocol we made  clear it would harm Northern Ireland it  would create instability and so it has  and I welcome the commitment now of the  EU to negotiate a new solution I welcome  the fact that the EU is now at the  negotiating table why is that David it's  because of the pressure we have put on  it's because of the steps the dup has  taken that finally we're getting a  negotiation a proper negotiation on  finding a solution to this problem  that's what I've asked for and now we  need to see that solution coming forward  and frankly the sooner the better is  Rishi sunak the dup's worst nightmare  because he clearly doesn't want to trade  war with the EU and therefore isn't  going to tear up the protocol well look  I do want to trade war with the EU I  want to trade with the EU but I  fundamentally want the ability to trade  within my own country and that is what  the protocol denies us and look whoever  is the prime minister in Westminster I  have to work with them I have to work  with the government and I will I am  absolutely committed every single day to  working to find this solution and I will  work with Rishi sunak to get that  solution and all I ask of the government  is that the objectives that they have  outlined in the Northern Ireland  protocol bill which is currently before  parliament are the objectives that they  pursue in the negotiations Liz truss  gave us that commitment Rishi sunak has  also stated that he wants to achieve the  same objectives so let's get on with it  let's get this Solution that's see the  negotiations and the tempo of those  negotiations increased so that we get to  an outcome that unionists can support  the refusal to restore power sharing  isn't really about the protocol or is it  about the fact that Shin fein's Michelle  O'Neill is on course to become the first  nationalist first Minister well look my  party is the Democratic unionist party  that means first of all we accept the  outcome of the election but let's not  forget David I withdrew my ministers  from the executive or the first Minister  back in February well before the  election I signaled way back in  September last year the the actions that  we would take if a solution was not  found on the protocol so this is not to  do with who is the first or Deputy first  Minister this is about the second part  of what my party stands for the Union  it is about unionists feeling that they  are they have been made second-class  citizens as a result of a protocol that  diminishes and undermines our place in  the United Kingdom that's what we need  to resolve and I'm focused on getting a  solution and this when we get that  solution I am absolutely clear the dup  will be back in government doing our bit  to build a better future for everyone in  Northern Ireland every time unionism has  pulled out of government it has ended up  in a worse situation if there is no deal  to restore power sharing at the end of  all of this what is the alternative  surely we can't just be looking at a  return to direct rule from Westminster  after all these years I'm not prepared  to accept failure I'm going to work for  Success I'm going to work for a solution  and every single day I'm going to be at  Westminster or at storm or in Brussels  wherever I need to be to press for that  solution because we need that for  Northern Ireland we need stability we've  got got a cost of 